            text: 'Explain how science and technology have improved the diagnosis and treatment of lung cancer. (12 marks)',
            model:
              "Science and technology have dramatically improved the diagnosis of lung cancer by moving far beyond the traditional chest X-ray, which was often too unclear to catch tumours early and frequently mistook cancer for other minor lung conditions. Today, patients are given a CT scan, which uses injected dye to build a highly detailed 3D picture of the lungs, allowing doctors to spot tumours when they are still as small as a pea. If a tumour is found, a PET-CT scan can track how much energy it is using, helping doctors judge exactly how active and dangerous it is. Doctors also use a bronchoscope, a flexible camera passed into the lungs, to take a direct tissue sample (a biopsy) to confirm whether a growth is cancerous.<br><br>This technological revolution in diagnosis has been matched by significant advances in treatment. If lung cancer is caught early, surgeons can now operate to remove the tumour or, in severe cases, the entire affected lung. Radiotherapy uses concentrated beams of radiation aimed directly at a tumour to shrink it, while chemotherapy uses powerful drugs injected into the bloodstream to destroy cancer cells throughout the body, either before surgery or to target any cells that remain afterwards. The most cutting-edge treatment, immunotherapy, works by boosting the patient's own immune system so that it can recognise and attack cancer cells itself.<br><br>Together, these diagnostic and treatment technologies mean that lung cancer, once almost impossible to detect before it was fatal, can now be identified far earlier and treated with a combination of precisely targeted therapies. However, because early symptoms are often mild or absent, many cases are still diagnosed too late for a cure, which is why the government has placed such heavy emphasis on prevention alongside these technological advances.",
