          answer:
            '<p><strong>Source A is highly useful for an enquiry into the state of medical technology because it demonstrates that aseptic surgery was already a fully established, routine practice in major civilian hospitals by 1913.</strong> The content reveals that surgical staff had "fully adopted aseptic technique," using scrubbing and steam autoclaves, and that postoperative infection had become "a rare occurrence." This is useful because it shows a genuinely high standard of surgical safety existed before the war began.</p><p>From my own knowledge, I can support this: by 1900, aseptic surgery (washing hands and faces, wearing sterilised rubber gloves and gowns, and using autoclaves invented by Charles Chamberland in 1881) had become the accepted standard in British hospitals, dramatically reducing fatal postoperative infections compared to Lister\'s earlier antiseptic methods.</p><p>The provenance of Source A enhances its utility; as an official hospital annual report, it was compiled by medical staff for governors and subscribers, giving it a professional incentive to be factually accurate about clinical standards, though it may also have wished to present the hospital favourably.</p><p><strong>Source B is also highly useful because it reveals the serious practical limitations of X-ray technology just one year before the outbreak of war.</strong> It shows that machines were "cumbersome," fragile, could not be moved easily, and that a single image could take up to ninety minutes.</p><p>From my own knowledge, I know that X-rays, discovered by Wilhelm Roentgen in 1895, were quickly adopted for diagnosis, but the radiation doses used were around 1,500 times stronger than today, causing burns and hair loss, and the equipment was far too heavy and slow for a fast-moving environment. This source is therefore extremely useful for explaining why, when war broke out in 1914, existing medical technology was not yet suited to the demands of a mobile battlefield.</p><p>The provenance of Source B, a contemporary medical textbook intended to train doctors, makes it a reliable, professionally-written account of the genuine technical limitations of the equipment, rather than an exaggerated or sensationalised claim.</p><p><strong>In conclusion, both sources are highly useful together.</strong> Source A shows that British medicine had reached an impressively high standard of surgical safety by 1913, while Source B shows that key diagnostic technology was still too slow, heavy, and fragile to be practical outside a stable hospital setting, helping to explain why the unique conditions of the Western Front would pose such severe new challenges.</p>',
