          text: "**Significance of the Terrain, Transport and Communications Infrastructure**\n\nConstant artillery shelling destroyed the region's roads and natural drainage systems. This left the landscape heavily cratered and filled with deep, waterlogged mud.\n\nHeavy motor ambulances frequently got permanently stuck in the deep mud, meaning the army had to rely heavily on horse-drawn ambulance wagons, which often required six horses to pull them through the sludge. These wagons were violently bumpy and often worsened severe injuries.\n\nStretcher-bearers (working in teams of four to six) faced the exhausting, dangerous task of carrying the wounded through muddy shell-holes in No Man's Land, often under heavy fire or in the dark. Once inside the trenches, it was incredibly difficult to manoeuvre stretchers due to crowded troops moving in opposite directions.\n\nProblems with communications infrastructure severely compounded transport difficulties. Constant artillery bombardments severed buried telephone lines, cutting off frontline medical posts from Casualty Clearing Stations. Consequently, medical officers had to rely on runners, runner-dogs, or homing pigeons to send messages. Runners were frequently killed or delayed in shell craters, leaving rear hospitals with no advance warning of mass casualties and causing severe bottlenecks where wounded men lay waiting in cold mud for hours. The devastated terrain and crippled communications severely slowed evacuation; this delay caused men to die from blood loss or shock, and allowed deep infections like gas gangrene to take hold before they could reach surgical treatment.",
