          text: '**The Thomas Splint**\n\nIn 1914 and 1915, a soldier with a gunshot or shrapnel wound that fractured their femur (thigh bone) only had a 20% chance of survival. Traditional splints did not keep the leg straight, meaning the jagged bone caused massive internal bleeding and shock during bumpy transport.\n\nIn December 1915, Robert Jones introduced the Thomas Splint (a device originally designed by his uncle, Hugh Thomas) to the Western Front.\n\nThis splint rigidly pulled the leg lengthways, completely stopping the broken bones from grinding on each other. The introduction of the Thomas Splint was one of the greatest medical successes of the war. By keeping the leg perfectly still, it prevented fatal blood loss and shock, miraculously increasing the survival rate for thigh fractures from 20% to 82%.',
