          '<strong>Model Answer:</strong><br><br><strong>Source A is highly useful for an enquiry into medical advances because it provides direct, statistical, and clinical evidence of the impact of the Thomas splint.</strong> The content of Source A highlights a dramatic reduction in fatalities, noting that before the splint\'s introduction, soldiers with shattered thigh bones regularly arrived in "terminal shock" due to blood loss caused by bones grating internally during transit. It shows that by keeping the leg rigid, the splint stabilized the injury. This is extremely useful because it provides a comparative look at medical outcomes before and after the development. The provenance of Source A is an official clinical report written by a practicing RAMC surgeon. Its purpose was to document medical outcomes for the army, meaning Captain Vaughan had an incentive to be highly precise with his clinical facts and figures, making the source highly reliable and useful for a historian.<br><br>From my own contextual knowledge, I can verify that in 1914 and 1915, a compound fracture of the femur carried a mortality rate of approximately 80%, primarily due to the severe bleeding and shock caused by transport in rough, horse-drawn ambulances. The introduction of the Thomas splint (originally developed by Hugh Owen Thomas and brought to the Western Front by Robert Jones in late 1915) successfully kept the leg under traction and rigid, dropping the mortality rate to just 20% by late 1916.<br><br><strong>Source B is also highly useful because it provides an incredibly detailed, clear illustration of the physical design and mechanical function of the Thomas splint.</strong> The photograph clearly shows the leather ring fitted against the groin and the steel rods running down the leg to provide the tension required to keep the bone aligned. This content is useful because it shows exactly how the apparatus was constructed and applied to a patient on a standard stretcher.<br><br>The provenance of Source B, being an official medical instruction manual published in 1917, means it was designed to train thousands of orderlies and stretcher-bearers rapidly as the splint was rolled out on a mass scale. While the studio setting is staged and does not reflect the muddy, chaotic reality of a frontline Regimental Aid Post, it remains highly useful for demonstrating the exact, standardized method of treatment. From my own knowledge, I know that the training of frontline staff in this method was a key reason why the splint was so effective; stretcher bearers applied the splint directly in the frontline trenches, preventing shock before the patient was even moved to a Dressing Station or Casualty Clearing Station.<br><br><strong>In conclusion, both sources are highly valuable.</strong> Source A provides the clinical justification and survival statistics proving the splint\'s medical effectiveness, while Source B provides the technical, physical layout showing exactly how this life-saving traction was achieved.',
        provenance_clue:
          'Source A is a clinical report by a surgeon; does this make its statistics reliable? Source B is a staged training photograph; while not taken on the chaotic frontline, does this actually make it better for demonstrating how the device technically worked?',
